\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

Step-independent training collapses while episode-level training succeeds; a factorial design separates the two mechanisms; and window size $h$ modulates severity.

\subsection{Setup}
\label{sec:setup}

We evaluate on ALFWorld~\cite{shridhar2021alfworld}, a text-based household environment with six task types requiring sequential reasoning over up to 50 steps. Reward is binary: $R \in \{0, r_+\}$. The base policy is Qwen3-1.7B fine-tuned on 2{,}546 expert demonstrations (SFT baseline: 12.5\% in-distribution, 0\% pre-SFT). At $p{=}0.125$ with $K{=}8$, Proposition~2 predicts ${\sim}80.9\%$ of gradient updates for locally correct windows carry incorrect sign. All conditions use RAGEN~\cite{ragen2025} (v0.1, accessed May 2026)\footnote{Code available at \url{https://github.com/RAGEN-AI/RAGEN}.} with GRPO~\cite{shao2024deepseekmath} ($K{=}8$, lr$=10^{-6}$, $\beta{=}0.01$, $\gamma{=}0.95$, 15 steps; full hyperparameters in Appendix~\ref{app:hyperparameters}). We report success rate on a 134-task OOD set (primary) and 32-task in-distribution set (secondary). Seed counts are noted per condition.

\subsection{Factorial Mechanism Separation}
\label{sec:factorial_experiments}

We implement the $2 \times 2$ factorial (Section~\ref{sec:factorial_design}): \textbf{Cell~A} (both mechanisms active; $h{=}2$, step-level advantage), \textbf{Cell~B} (M1 only; $h{=}2$, episode-level advantage), and \textbf{Cell~D} (neither; full trajectory, episode-level advantage). Two seeds each. The fourth cell---\textbf{Cell~C} (full observation, step-level advantage)---would isolate M2 without M1; we predict partial improvement over Cell~A, since removing information loss enables gradient alignment but step-level advantage may still introduce noise. We attempted this configuration but encountered persistent training instability (Appendix~\ref{app:cell_c}).

\subsection{Results}
\label{sec:results}

Cell~A collapses in two phases (Table~\ref{tab:main_results}): task success drops to 0\% within 3--9 steps while action validity initially remains above 80\%, then continued sign misattribution destroys formatting (validity: 89.3\% $\to$ 43.1\% between steps 2--4, reaching 0.06\% by step~15).% exp_id: hsweep_h2_seed1, hsweep_h2_seed2
Cell~B eliminates sign misattribution while retaining information loss: training success reaches 55\% (seed~2), but OOD generalization is bounded by the information-loss ceiling (best OOD: 5.97\% at step~5). Both seeds converge to the same OOD ceiling (0.75\% at step~15) despite different training trajectories (20\% vs.\ 55\%), confirming the bottleneck is architectural rather than statistical.% exp_id: factorial_h2_episodeadv_seed1, factorial_h2_episodeadv_seed2

Cell~D reaches 17.2\% mean OOD success (range: 10.4--23.9\%) with monotonic improvement across both seeds, the only condition that substantially exceeds the SFT baseline.% exp_id: exp029_episode_level, exp029_seed3_ood_v2

The A$\to$B comparison isolates M2: eliminating sign misattribution converts catastrophic collapse into bounded learning. The B$\to$D comparison isolates M1: removing the information-loss ceiling enables OOD generalization ($+$11.2pp mean). Both mechanisms are necessary to explain the full failure pattern. Figure~\ref{fig:hsweep}(a) visualizes these trajectories.

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig_training_dynamics.pdf}
\caption{(a)~Training dynamics across factorial conditions (metric type indicated in legend). Cell~A collapses within 5 steps; Cell~B shows bounded learning; Cell~D improves monotonically. (b)~Window size $h$ vs.\ OOD success (seed~1; cf.\ Table~\ref{tab:main_results} for two-seed mean). A credit assignment threshold between $h{=}10$ and $h{=}20$ separates collapse from recovery. Hatched bars indicate collapsed conditions.}
\label{fig:hsweep}
\end{figure*}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{1.7B results on ALFWorld (best checkpoint per seed, evaluated on held-out test sets). In-Dist: 32-task set; OOD: 134-task set. Cell~A reports best seed; Cell~B reports best checkpoint (step~5). Cell~D reports mean of two seeds (In-Dist range: 28.1--40.6; OOD range: 10.4--23.9).}
\label{tab:main_results}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{@{}llcc@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Condition} & \textbf{Mechanisms} & \textbf{In-Dist (\%)} & \textbf{OOD (\%)} \\
\midrule
SFT baseline & --- & 12.50 & 6.72 \\
Cell A ($h{=}2$, step) & M1+M2 & 0 & 0 \\
Cell B ($h{=}2$, episode) & M1 only & 18.75 & 5.97 \\
Cell D (full, episode) & neither & \textbf{34.38} & \textbf{17.16} \\
\midrule
Dense reward & M1+M2 & 0 & 0 \\
REINFORCE++ & neither & 12.50 & --- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
% exp_id: hsweep_h2_seed1, factorial_h2_episodeadv_seed1, exp029_episode_level, exp029_seed3_v2, exp013f, gae_baseline_seed1
\end{table}

\paragraph{Additional conditions.} Dense reward, REINFORCE++, and discount-factor sweep experiments all confirm that sign misattribution---not reward sparsity or discounting---drives the failure (Appendix~\ref{app:additional}).

\paragraph{Window-size sweep.} A credit assignment threshold between $h{=}10$ and $h{=}20$ separates collapse from recovery (Figure~\ref{fig:hsweep}b; seed~1): $h{=}2$: 0\%, $h{=}5$: 0\%, $h{=}10$: 6.25\%, $h{=}20$: 15.6\%, episode-level: 23.88\%. This threshold is observed in a single seed; multi-seed verification is needed to confirm its robustness. Only episode-level training achieves substantial generalization.% exp_id: h_sweep_series, exp029_seed1
\label{sec:results_hsweep}

\subsection{Sign Misattribution Verification}
\label{sec:sign_verification}

We operationalize local correctness (Definition~1) through episode outcome: in a successful episode, all windows with valid actions are labeled locally correct. The credit agreement rate---the fraction of such windows where GRPO's advantage sign is positive---is 52.07\% ($n{=}3{,}000$ windows; 95\% CI [50.3\%, 53.9\%]) under step-independent training ($h{=}2$), marginally above random chance ($p{=}0.025$ vs.\ 50\%; $+$2.07pp effect size). Advantage sign correlates near-perfectly with episode outcome ($r{>}0.99$) but shows near-zero dependence on local quality (point biserial $r{=}0.068$). Combined with $\gamma{=}1.0$ collapse (Appendix~\ref{app:additional}), these measurements confirm sign misattribution as the causal driver. This operationalization is a conservative approximation: successful episodes may contain suboptimal windows, making our label an upper bound on true local correctness. Even under this generous criterion, credit agreement remains near-random, strengthening the diagnosis.% exp_id: 7b_cell_a_h2

\subsection{Episode-Level Training: Seed Variability}
\label{sec:seed_variability}

Episode-level GRPO addresses the dual-mechanism failure, but training outcomes remain seed-dependent. We systematically evaluate $N{=}11$ random seeds under identical hyperparameters, revealing a $2{\times}2$ structure: \textit{training stability} (collapsed vs.\ non-collapsed) $\times$ \textit{OOD generalization} (stagnant vs.\ generalized).

We define \textbf{collapsed} seeds as those where in-distribution success declines $>$50\% from peak and remains below baseline for $\geq$3 consecutive steps. \textbf{Non-collapsed} seeds are the complement. Among non-collapsed seeds, we further distinguish \textbf{generalized} (Fisher exact $p{<}0.01$ vs.\ SFT on the 134-task OOD set) from \textbf{stagnant} (not significant vs.\ SFT). Table~\ref{tab:seed_variability} reports results for each seed at its best-performing checkpoint.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Episode-level GRPO across 11 seeds (134 OOD tasks, $\tau{=}0.4$, best checkpoint per seed). Category: C=Collapsed, S=Non-collapsed (stagnant), G=Generalized (Fisher exact $p{<}0.01$ vs.\ SFT). Wilson 95\% CI. SFT baseline: 6.72\% (9/134). Seed2 achieves $p{=}0.02$, falling just outside the conservative $p{<}0.01$ threshold, and is classified as Stagnant. Step-5 results for all seeds in Appendix~\ref{app:step5_unified}.}
\label{tab:seed_variability}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{@{}llcccc@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Seed} & \textbf{Step} & \textbf{Cat.} & \textbf{OOD (\%)} & \textbf{95\% CI} & \textbf{vs SFT} \\
\midrule
seed2048 & 5 & C & 8.21 & [4.6, 14.2] & NS \\
seed42 & 5 & C & 12.69 & [8.0, 19.4] & NS \\
seed8 & 5 & C & 13.43 & [8.6, 20.3] & NS \\
\midrule
seed200 & 5 & S & 10.45 & [6.3, 16.8] & NS \\
seed300 & 5 & S & 10.45 & [6.3, 16.8] & NS \\
seed100 & 5 & S & 11.19 & [6.9, 17.6] & NS \\
seed100 & 10 & S & 12.69 & [8.0, 19.4] & NS \\
seed2 & 15 & S & 15.67 & [10.5, 22.8] & $p{=}0.02$ \\
\midrule
seed1 & 5 & G$^\dagger$ & 23.88 & [17.4, 31.8] & $p{<}0.001$ \\
seed5 & 10 & G & 25.37 & [18.8, 33.3] & $p{<}0.001$ \\
seed6 & 15 & G & 29.10 & [22.2, 37.1] & $p{<}0.001$ \\
seed4 & 15 & G & 30.60 & [23.5, 38.7] & $p{<}0.001$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\begin{flushleft}
\small $^\dagger$ RAGEN built-in eval; checkpoint unavailable for re-evaluation.
\end{flushleft}
\end{table}

Three distinct outcomes emerge. 3/11 seeds collapse (27\%), with response length inflating to 14{,}000--16{,}000 tokens (Appendix~\ref{app:response_length}); 4/11 are non-collapsed but stagnant (36\%), with OOD indistinguishable from SFT (10.45--15.67\%, all NS at $\alpha{=}0.01$); and 4/11 generalize (36\%), reaching 23.88--30.60\% OOD ($p{<}0.001$). No collapsed seed generalizes, confirming stable training is necessary. One seed (seed2) shows delayed learning: 15.67\% OOD after 15 steps ($p{=}0.02$, borderline).% exp_id: exp029 multi-seed series

The stagnant--generalized gap is not explained by insufficient training: seed100 improves only 1.50pp from step~5 to step~10 (11.19\% $\to$ 12.69\%, both NS), while seed5 reaches 25.37\% at step~10. Stable training is necessary but not sufficient for OOD generalization---the mechanism driving seed-dependent transfer remains an open question (Section~\ref{sec:discussion}).

\subsection{Cross-Scale Verification}
\label{sec:cross_scale}

A single-seed experiment on Qwen3-8B provides preliminary evidence that both failure and fix persist at scale (Table~\ref{tab:8b_results}). Cell~A collapses within two steps (28.1\% $\to$ 1.5\%). Cell~D reaches 96.9\% in-distribution and 90.3\% OOD at step~10, exceeding the SFT baseline (80.6\% OOD) by 9.7pp. Restricting to $h{=}2$ drops in-distribution success from 68.75\% to 31.25\% (37.5pp gap). Single-seed due to Fully Sharded Data Parallel (FSDP) memory constraints; multi-seed verification is needed to confirm robustness.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{8B results on ALFWorld. SFT and $h{=}2$ use decoding temperature $\tau{=}0$; RL uses $\tau{=}0.4$. Cell~A: step~2 (post-collapse). Cell~D: step~10.}
\label{tab:8b_results}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lcc@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Method} & \textbf{In-Dist (\%)} & \textbf{OOD (\%)} \\
\midrule
SFT baseline & 68.75 & 80.60 \\
SFT $h{=}2$ & 31.25 & --- \\
Cell A: step-indep.\ ($h{=}2$) & 28.1 $\to$ 1.5 & --- \\
Cell D: episode-level & \textbf{96.88} & \textbf{90.30} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
% exp_id: 8b_cell_c_episode_v9d, 8b_v9d_ood_eval_v5
\end{table}

In this single-seed experiment, the episode-level fix appears more effective at 8B (90.3\% OOD vs.\ 17.2\% mean at 1.7B; Figure~\ref{fig:cross_scale}a), suggesting larger models may have more capacity unlocked by correct credit assignment.

\subsection{Cross-Environment Validation}
\label{sec:cross_env}

Step-independent training collapses on ScienceWorld~\cite{wang2022scienceworld} (30 task types, SFT baseline 30.1\% at $\tau{=}0.4$): Cell~A ($h{=}2$) drops to 0\% task success throughout all 15 steps, despite the higher baseline ($p{=}30.1\%$ vs.\ ALFWorld's $12.5\%$).% exp_id: scienceworld_grpo_cell_a, scienceworld_sft_eval_v2
Restricting context to $h{=}2$ drops SFT success from 34.8\% ($\tau{=}0$) to 4.5\% (30.3pp M1 gap), paralleling the 37.5pp gap at 8B ALFWorld (Figure~\ref{fig:cross_scale}b).% exp_id: sw_info_loss_bjb1
These single-seed results provide preliminary evidence that both mechanisms operate beyond ALFWorld; multi-seed and multi-environment verification would strengthen this claim.

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig_cross_scale.pdf}
\caption{(a)~Cross-scale verification (single seed): both collapse (Cell~A) and fix (Cell~D) are observed from 1.7B to 8B. The episode-level fix appears more effective at 8B (96.9\% vs.\ 34.4\% in-distribution). (b)~Information loss (M1) quantification (single seed each): restricting context to $h{=}2$ drops SFT performance by 37.5pp at 8B ALFWorld and 30.3pp on ScienceWorld.}
\label{fig:cross_scale}
\end{figure*}

