\documentclass[11pt]{article}

\usepackage[review]{acl}

\usepackage{times}
\usepackage{latexsym}
\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}
\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}
\usepackage{microtype}
\usepackage{graphicx}
\usepackage{booktabs}
\usepackage{amsmath}
\usepackage{amssymb}
\usepackage{amsthm}
\usepackage{enumitem}

\title{Sign Misattribution in Step-Independent Agent RL:\\Dual Mechanism Diagnosis and Episode-Level Correction}

\author{Anonymous}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Multi-turn agent reinforcement learning requires credit assignment across long interaction trajectories, yet current frameworks default to step-independent windowing that decomposes trajectories into independently optimized windows. This introduces a structural credit assignment pathology driven by two mechanisms: \textit{policy information loss}, which truncates context and imposes a constant performance ceiling, and \textit{advantage sign misattribution}, which causes gradient updates to reflect episode outcomes rather than local action quality, producing cumulative degradation. At $p{=}12.5\%$ with group size $K{=}8$, theory predicts 80.9\% of gradient updates for locally correct actions carry incorrect sign under binary reward; empirical measurement under penalty reward yields a near-random 52\% credit agreement rate ($p{=}0.025$ vs.\ 50\%; $+$2pp effect size). A $2{\times}2$ factorial separates the mechanisms: eliminating sign misattribution converts catastrophic collapse into bounded learning; removing information loss enables generalization. Episode-level Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO), treating the full trajectory as a single optimization sample, addresses both structural preconditions. A systematic 11-seed evaluation reveals that stable training is necessary but not sufficient: 73\% of seeds avoid collapse, but only 36\% achieve statistically significant out-of-distribution (OOD) generalization ($p{<}0.01$; 24--31\% OOD vs.\ 6.7\% supervised fine-tuning (SFT) baseline). Preliminary single-seed cross-scale verification at 8B shows both failure and fix (90.3\% OOD vs.\ collapse to 1.5\%), and cross-environment validation on ScienceWorld provides evidence the collapse extends beyond ALFWorld.
\end{abstract}

\input{introduction}
\input{method}
\input{theoretical_analysis}
\input{experiments}
\input{related_work}
\input{discussion}
\input{conclusion}

\section*{Limitations}

Our experiments span two environments (ALFWorld, ScienceWorld) and two scales (1.7B, 8B); additional environments and scales would strengthen generality. The 8B and ScienceWorld results report single-seed runs due to computational constraints. Among 11 seeds at 1.7B, only 36\% achieve statistically significant OOD generalization; we cannot rule out that stagnant seeds would eventually generalize with substantially more training steps, though seed100's minimal improvement from step~5 to step~10 suggests the bottleneck is not simply training duration. The mechanism driving seed-dependent transfer remains an open question. For very long episodes ($T > 100$) or tasks with many independent sub-goals, uniform episode-level advantage may limit efficiency even though it preserves direction correctness.

