\section{Theoretical Analysis}
\label{sec:theory}

Sign misattribution is the more fundamental failure mechanism because it causes active divergence, not merely slow convergence. We derive the probability of incorrect gradient direction below; full proofs appear in Appendix~\ref{app:theory_full}.

\textbf{Definition 1} (Locally correct action). \textit{An action $a_t$ is \textbf{locally correct} if it would receive positive advantage under an oracle that observes the full episode history $h_t$ and has access to the true action-value function. Equivalently, window $k$ contains locally correct actions if replacing them (holding all other windows fixed) would decrease the probability of episode success.}

\textbf{Proposition 1.} \textit{Under step-independent training with binary episode reward $R_{\text{episode}} \in \{0, r_+\}$, the probability that window $k$'s advantage $\hat{A}_k$ has the correct sign (aligned with local action quality) approaches the base success rate $p$ as the number of rollouts $K$ per group increases.}

\textit{Proof sketch.} In a failed episode ($R_{\text{episode}} = 0$), all windows receive $R_k = 0$. If any rollout in the group succeeded, $\mu > 0$, so every window in the failed episode receives $\hat{A}_k < 0$---including windows with locally correct actions. The probability of receiving the wrong sign in a failed episode is $1 - (1-p)^{K-1}$ (at least one group member succeeded). As $K$ grows, this approaches $1-p$, and the overall probability of correct sign approaches $p$. \hfill$\square$

The key distinction is temporal. Information loss (M1) imposes a constant ceiling determined by $h$; sign misattribution (M2) is cumulative---each wrong-sign gradient step erodes policy competence below that ceiling. A wrong-sign gradient moves the optimizer \textit{away} from good policies; a reduced-magnitude gradient from discounting ($\gamma^{(N-k)}$) still points toward good policies. Sign errors produce active divergence; magnitude errors merely slow convergence (Appendix~\ref{app:sign_fundamental}).

\textbf{Proposition 2.}\label{prop:correct_sign} \textit{Let $p$ denote the episode success rate and $K$ the number of rollouts per group. Under step-independent training with binary episode reward, the fraction of gradient updates with correct sign is:}
\begin{equation}
P(\text{correct} \mid \text{update}) = \frac{p}{1 - (1-p)^{K}}
\label{eq:correct_conditional}
\end{equation}

\textit{Derivation.} Three mutually exclusive cases determine gradient direction for a window with locally correct actions: (1)~episode succeeded (probability $p$): correct sign; (2)~episode failed but group contains a success (probability $(1{-}p)[1{-}(1{-}p)^{K-1}]$): wrong sign; (3)~entire group failed (probability $(1{-}p)^K$): zero gradient. Conditioning on non-zero gradient (Cases 1 and 2) yields Equation~\ref{eq:correct_conditional}.

At $p{=}12.5\%$, $K{=}8$, only 19.1\% of gradient updates have the correct sign (Figure~\ref{fig:theory_curve}). In the large-$K$ limit, $P(\text{correct} \mid \text{update}) \to p$: ${\sim}87.5\%$ of updates for locally correct windows point in the wrong direction.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig_theory_curve.pdf}
\caption{Probability of correct gradient sign as a function of episode success rate $p$ (Proposition~2). At typical agent task success rates ($p < 0.2$, shaded), the majority of gradient updates carry incorrect sign regardless of group size $K$. At ALFWorld's baseline ($p{=}12.5\%$, $K{=}8$), only 19.1\% of updates are correct. The dashed diagonal shows the large-$K$ limit $P_{\text{correct}} \to p$.}
\label{fig:theory_curve}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Severity scales with task difficulty.} Sign misattribution becomes \textit{more} severe as the task gets harder (Equation~\ref{eq:correct_conditional}): at $p{=}50\%$, $P(\text{correct} \mid \text{update}) = 50.2\%$---barely above random; at $p{=}5\%$, only 5.1\% of updates are correct. Multi-turn agent tasks with low baseline success rates are precisely where credit assignment matters most, yet where step-independent training produces the worst gradient signal. Increasing $K$ does not help: more updates occur (fewer all-fail groups), but each remains equally likely to carry the wrong sign.

\paragraph{Reward saturation.} When all $K$ rollouts receive the same binary reward, $\sigma = 0$ and $\hat{A}_k = 0$, producing zero gradient. At $p{=}12.5\%$, $K{=}8$, the all-fail probability is $(1{-}p)^K \approx 34.4\%$; combined with the negligible all-success case ($p^K < 10^{-7}$), roughly one-third of batches yield no gradient at all. This compounds sign misattribution: of the ${\sim}66\%$ of batches that produce a gradient, only 19.1\% have the correct sign. The effective learning rate is $0.66 \times 0.19 \approx 12.5\%$ of nominal---well below the regime where convergence guarantees hold.

\paragraph{Cumulative divergence.} Sign misattribution produces a systematic gradient bias. Let $\theta^*$ denote an optimal policy parameterization. At each update, the expected gradient under step-independent training is:
\begin{equation}
\mathbb{E}[\nabla_\theta \mathcal{L}] = P_c \cdot g_+ + (1 - P_c) \cdot g_-
\label{eq:biased_gradient}
\end{equation}
where $P_c = P(\text{correct} \mid \text{update})$, $g_+$ points toward $\theta^*$, and $g_-$ points away. Empirical measurement from training logs confirms approximately comparable per-group advantage magnitudes ($|\hat{A}_{\text{correct}}|/|\hat{A}_{\text{wrong}}| \approx 1.32$). When $P_c < 0.5$ (which holds for $p < 0.5$; Equation~\ref{eq:correct_conditional}), the expected gradient points weakly away from good policies. The cumulative effect of this directional bias, amplified by the M1--M2 positive feedback loop (Section~\ref{sec:dual_mechanism}), drives progressive policy degradation: information loss (M1) caps $p$; lower $p$ increases wrong-sign frequency under M2, which further depresses $p$, producing the empirically observed collapse.

\appendix

\section{Theoretical Analysis: Full Derivations}
\label{app:theory_full}

\subsection{Why Sign Misattribution is the Core Mechanism}
\label{app:sign_fundamental}

The two mechanisms identified in Section~\ref{sec:dual_mechanism} operate at different layers and produce qualitatively different failures. Policy information loss (Mechanism 1) operates at the inference layer, restricting the observable context and establishing a structural performance ceiling that is constant across training. Sign misattribution (Mechanism 2) operates at the training layer, corrupting gradient directions and progressively degrading performance below this ceiling.

\textit{Proof of Proposition 1.} Define ``correct sign'' as follows: for a window containing locally beneficial actions, the correct sign of $\hat{A}_k$ is positive (reinforcement); for a window containing locally detrimental actions, the correct sign is negative (suppression). Under binary reward, the return for window $k$ is $R_k = \gamma^{(N-k)} \cdot R_{\text{episode}}$ (Equation~\ref{eq:step_return}). In a failed episode ($R_{\text{episode}} = 0$), all windows receive $R_k = 0$ regardless of their local action quality. If the group contains at least one successful rollout, the group mean $\mu > 0$, and all windows in the failed episode receive negative advantages after normalization via Equation~\ref{eq:grpo_advantage}. This includes windows where the agent took locally correct actions. Now consider a window with locally correct actions. In a successful episode (probability $p$), the window receives positive $\hat{A}_k$, which is the correct sign. In a failed episode (probability $1 - p$), the window receives negative $\hat{A}_k$ whenever $\mu > 0$, which is the wrong sign. The probability that $\mu = 0$ (all $K$ rollouts in the group failed) is $(1-p)^K$, which shrinks exponentially with $K$. As $K$ grows, nearly every failed episode produces a non-zero group mean, and the window receives the wrong sign with probability approaching $1-p$. The probability of correct sign therefore approaches $p$. \hfill$\square$

The distinction between sign error and magnitude error further clarifies why Mechanism 2 is catastrophic while Mechanism 1 is merely suboptimal. The discount factor $\gamma^{(N-k)}$ in Equation~\ref{eq:step_return} introduces magnitude decay across window positions: for an episode with $T=50$ steps and $h=2$, the first window ($k=1$) retains $\gamma^{N-1} = 0.95^{24} \approx 0.29$ of the terminal window's return. This attenuation reduces the gradient magnitude for early windows but preserves the gradient direction. A gradient with correct sign and reduced magnitude produces slow convergence, a condition recoverable through longer training. A gradient with wrong sign produces active divergence: the optimization pushes the policy away from good behaviors and toward bad ones.

\subsection{Formal Probability Analysis}
\label{app:formal_probability}

\textit{Derivation of Proposition 2.} For a window with locally correct actions, the gradient direction depends on $\text{sign}(\hat{A}_k)$, which depends on whether the episode succeeded and whether the group contains any successful rollouts. Three mutually exclusive cases arise.

\textit{Case 1: Episode succeeded} (probability $p$). The window receives $R_k = \gamma^{(N-k)} \cdot r_+ > 0$. With at least one failed rollout in the group, $R_k > \mu$ after normalization, producing $\hat{A}_k > 0$.\footnote{The edge case where all $K$ rollouts succeed (probability $p^K$) is negligible at the baseline success rates considered ($p^8 < 10^{-7}$ at $p = 12.5\%$).} The gradient reinforces the window's actions. For a window with locally correct actions, this is the correct direction.

\textit{Case 2: Episode failed, group contains at least one success} (probability $(1-p)[1 - (1-p)^{K-1}]$). The window receives $R_k = 0 < \mu$, producing $\hat{A}_k < 0$. The gradient suppresses the window's actions. For a window with locally correct actions, this is the wrong direction.

\textit{Case 3: Episode failed, entire group failed} (probability $(1-p) \cdot (1-p)^{K-1} = (1-p)^{K}$). All returns are zero, so $\mu = 0$ and $\sigma = 0$. The advantage is zero, producing no gradient update.

Summing Cases~1 and~3 yields $P(\text{correct sign or no update}) = p + (1-p)^{K}$. Since only Cases~1 and~2 produce non-zero gradients, the conditional probability follows as Equation~\ref{eq:correct_conditional}.

\textit{Remark.} Proposition~2 assumes binary episode rewards for analytical tractability. Under non-binary rewards (e.g., with per-step penalties for invalid actions), the group mean $\mu$ is nonzero even when all episodes fail, but the qualitative conclusion is preserved: advantage sign is determined by relative episode performance within the group, not by local action quality within individual windows. Section~\ref{sec:sign_verification} validates this empirically using the full reward structure.

\textit{Penalty reward effect on credit agreement.} Proposition~2 predicts 19.1\% correct-sign rate (i.e., 80.9\% incorrect) under pure binary reward. Our experiments include a $-0.1$ penalty per invalid action, making the reward non-binary. This penalty introduces variance within failed episodes: windows with valid actions receive $R_k = 0$ while windows with invalid actions receive $R_k = -0.1h$. Within a failed episode, the penalty-induced variance can occasionally produce positive $\hat{A}_k$ for windows with valid actions even when $\mu > 0$, since the normalization accounts for intra-group variance. This shifts the empirical credit agreement from the theoretical 19.1\% toward 52\%---still marginally above the 50\% random baseline ($p{=}0.025$; $+$2pp effect size) and far below the level needed for convergence. A binomial test formally rejects $H_0{:}\, P = 0.191$ ($z = 45.9$, $p < 10^{-100}$), confirming that the gap between 19.1\% (binary theory) and 52\% (penalty-reward measurement) reflects the reward structure difference, not a failure of the theoretical framework.

We evaluate these expressions at the empirical baseline of our experiments. At $p = 12.5\%$ and $K = 8$: $(1-p)^K = 0.875^8 \approx 0.344$, giving $P(\text{correct or no update}) \approx 0.469$. Of the 65.6\% of cases that produce a gradient update, only $P(\text{correct} \mid \text{update}) = 0.125 / 0.656 \approx 19.1\%$ have the correct sign. As $K$ increases to 16, $(1-p)^{K} \approx 0.118$, and $P(\text{correct} \mid \text{update}) \approx 14.2\%$. In the large-$K$ limit, $P(\text{correct} \mid \text{update}) \to p$, so approximately $1 - p = 87.5\%$ of all gradient updates for locally correct windows point in the wrong direction.

\subsection{Why the Community Defaults to Step-Independent Training}
\label{app:why_defaults}

If step-independent training is structurally flawed for multi-turn agents, why has it become the default? We identify six contributing factors.

\textbf{1. Context length constraints.} Smaller language models (1B to 4B parameters) typically support context lengths of 2048 to 4096 tokens. Multi-turn episodes spanning 50 or more interaction steps easily exceed these limits. Step-independent windowing is a pragmatic response to a hardware constraint, not a deliberate algorithmic choice.

\textbf{2. Token-level training inertia.} Language model training is organized around predicting the next token over fixed-length sequences. When multi-turn RL inherits this paradigm, the natural unit of optimization becomes a fixed-length window rather than a variable-length episode. The mismatch between token-level optimization and episode-level credit assignment goes unquestioned because it aligns with existing training infrastructure.

\textbf{3. Inherited framework default.} The RAGEN framework~\cite{ragen2025} implements step-independent mode as its default configuration, not as one option among alternatives. Users inherit this choice without an explicit decision point, and default configurations propagate silently through downstream projects.

\textbf{4. Memory and compute constraints.} Episode-level training requires processing $O(T)$ tokens per sequence, incurring $O(T^2)$ attention cost with full self-attention. Step-independent training reduces this to $O(h)$ tokens and $O(h^2)$ attention cost per window. The computational savings are immediate and measurable, while the credit assignment failure is subtle and deferred until training collapse occurs.

\textbf{5. Reward shaping as apparent fix.} Dense reward shaping (per-step bonuses for intermediate goals) appears to address sparse reward problems. Our dense reward experiment (Section~\ref{sec:results_dense}) reveals a different failure mode: the agent maximizes format-level rewards (action validity rises from 60\% to 97.5\%) while task success remains at 0\%. Reward shaping does not resolve sign misattribution; it creates new optimization targets that the agent can game without solving the underlying task.

\textbf{6. Absence of negative results.} When step-independent training fails, practitioners attribute the failure to insufficient hyperparameter tuning or the inherent difficulty of sparse rewards. Without published analyses that isolate the credit assignment mechanism as the root cause, the community cannot distinguish between ``this problem is hard'' and ``this training paradigm is broken for this class of problems.''

\subsection{Connection to Classical Credit Assignment}
\label{app:classical_connection}

Our findings connect to a long line of work on temporal credit assignment in reinforcement learning.

\textbf{Temporal difference learning and eligibility traces.} Sutton's TD($\lambda$)~\cite{sutton1988td} provides a spectrum of credit assignment strategies parameterized by $\lambda \in [0,1]$. At $\lambda = 0$, credit is assigned via one-step bootstrapped returns. At $\lambda = 1$, TD($\lambda$) reduces to Monte Carlo returns. Step-independent training falls below this spectrum: windows are disconnected from each other, so temporal propagation of credit does not occur.

\textbf{Generalized Advantage Estimation.} GAE($\lambda, \gamma$)~\cite{schulman2016gae} extends TD($\lambda$) with a separate discount factor. GAE(1, 1) produces undiscounted Monte Carlo returns and is equivalent to episode-level GRPO without a critic. The key difference is that GAE uses value function subtraction as the baseline, while GRPO uses group normalization.

\textbf{REINFORCE.} Williams' REINFORCE algorithm~\cite{williams1992reinforce} computes policy gradients from complete episode returns with a learned baseline $b(s)$. Episode-level GRPO is equivalent to REINFORCE where the group mean $\mu$ replaces $b(s)$ and the group standard deviation $\sigma$ provides normalization. The algorithmic structure is not new. Our contribution is the diagnosis: the community's standard adaptation of this algorithm for multi-turn LLM agents (step-independent windowing) silently breaks the credit assignment structure that REINFORCE assumes.

\textbf{Multi-agent credit assignment analogy.} Step-independent windows can be understood through cooperative multi-agent games. Each window acts as an independent ``agent'' sharing a team reward but optimized without knowledge of other windows' actions. The sign misattribution problem parallels team reward credit assignment in cooperative multi-agent RL: when the team fails, all agents receive identical penalties regardless of individual contribution. In multi-agent RL, this is a recognized problem with known solutions such as counterfactual baselines and value decomposition methods.

\section{Hyperparameters}
\label{app:hyperparameters}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Shared RL training hyperparameters across all conditions.}
\label{tab:hyperparameters_app}
\begin{tabular}{lc}
\toprule
Parameter & Value \\
\midrule
Group size $K$ & 8 \\
Learning rate & $1 \times 10^{-6}$ \\
KL penalty $\beta$ & 0.01 \\
Discount $\gamma$ & 0.95 \\
Invalid action penalty & 0.1 \\
Tasks per training step & 16 \\
Training steps & 15 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

All 1.7B experiments were conducted on a single NVIDIA A100 80\,GB GPU. Each training run (15 steps, $K{=}8$ rollouts per task, 16 tasks per step) required approximately 6--8 GPU-hours. The 11-seed evaluation totaled ${\sim}$80 GPU-hours. The 8B experiment used 4$\times$ A100 80\,GB with FSDP, requiring ${\sim}$24 GPU-hours per run. ScienceWorld experiments used the same single-GPU setup as the 1.7B ALFWorld runs.

\paragraph{SFT baseline.} The base policy was fine-tuned on 2{,}546 expert demonstrations from the ALFWorld training split following the protocol of RAGEN~\cite{ragen2025}, using learning rate $2 \times 10^{-5}$, batch size~16, and 3 epochs.

\section{Additional Conditions}
\label{app:additional}

All conditions use single seeds unless noted.

\paragraph{Dense reward.} Step-independent $h{=}2$ with $+0.15$ per valid action. Action validity rises from 60\% to 97.5\% while task success remains 0\%---the agent games format-level rewards without solving tasks (Figure~\ref{fig:additional_conditions}b).
\label{sec:results_dense}

\paragraph{REINFORCE++.} Episode-level with per-token running-mean baseline (no critic). REINFORCE++ matches the SFT baseline (12.50\%) without improvement, suggesting that per-token advantage normalization alone does not help when the per-token baseline lacks a learned value function. We note that 15 training steps may be insufficient for REINFORCE++ to converge; however, the Cell~A collapse pattern represents active degradation rather than mere undertraining, as performance drops below the SFT initialization.

\paragraph{Discount factor sweep.} $\gamma \in \{0.50, 0.80, 0.95, 1.00\}$ at $h{=}5$. All four collapse to 0\% within 5 steps, including $\gamma{=}1.0$ where no magnitude decay exists. This confirms sign misattribution, not discounting, is the operative mechanism (Figure~\ref{fig:additional_conditions}a).

\paragraph{Cell~C attempt.}
\label{app:cell_c}
Cell~C (full observation + step-level advantage) would isolate M2 without M1. We attempted this configuration across six training runs with varying hyperparameters (KL penalty $\beta \in \{0.001, 0.01, 0.04\}$, learning rate $\in \{5{\times}10^{-7}, 10^{-6}\}$). All runs terminated due to training instability: KL divergence spiked above 10.0 within 2--3 steps, triggering NaN losses. We attribute this to the combination of full-length sequences ($>$4096 tokens) with step-level advantage normalization, which produces extreme advantage magnitudes at early window positions. Cell~C remains an important gap in the factorial; addressing it likely requires architectural changes (e.g., gradient clipping per window position) beyond the scope of this diagnostic study.

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig_additional_conditions.pdf}
\caption{(a)~Standard remedies for step-independent training: dense reward shaping and discount factor $\gamma{=}1.0$ both collapse to 0\%; REINFORCE++ matches the SFT baseline without improvement. Only episode-level training (Cell~D) exceeds baseline. (b)~Dense reward gaming: action validity rises from 60\% to 97.5\% while task success remains 0\%---the agent maximizes format-level rewards without solving tasks.}
\label{fig:additional_conditions}
\end{figure*}

\section{Response Length During Training}
\label{app:response_length}

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig_response_length.pdf}
\caption{Response length over training for 7 seeds under episode-level GRPO. Collapsed seeds (red) exhibit length inflation to 14{,}000--16{,}000 tokens. Generalized seeds (green) maintain or reduce response length. Stagnant seed (orange, seed100) remains near the SFT initialization.}
\label{fig:response_length}
\end{figure}

\section{Unified Step-5 Evaluation}
\label{app:step5_unified}

To guard against cherry-picking of checkpoints, Table~\ref{tab:step5_unified} reports all 11 seeds at a fixed evaluation point (training step~5). OOD results are included only for seeds with checkpoints evaluated under the D134 protocol (Section~\ref{sec:setup}); remaining checkpoints were unavailable for post-hoc OOD evaluation.

