\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

Step-independent training in multi-turn agent RL fails through two mechanisms: policy information loss imposes a performance ceiling, while advantage sign misattribution causes cumulative degradation: at $p{=}12.5\%$ with $K{=}8$, Proposition~2 predicts ${\sim}$80.9\% incorrect-sign updates under binary reward (52\% measured under penalty reward, near-random credit assignment, $+$2pp above chance). A $2{\times}2$ factorial separates the mechanisms; episode-level GRPO addresses both (17.2\% mean OOD at 1.7B; preliminary single-seed 8B evidence shows 90.3\% OOD). A systematic 11-seed evaluation reveals that stable training is necessary but not sufficient: 73\% avoid collapse, but only 36\% generalize ($p{<}0.01$). The central insight---sign errors are more destructive than magnitude imprecision---applies to any agent RL method combining step-level windowing, sparse binary reward, and group-relative normalization.

